# Offsets 004h and 040h — the two registers with a layout that nothing decodes.
#
# HAND BUILT FROM THE SPECIFICATION. The capture only ever reads 0020h and
# 0030h.
#
# 040h matters more than the coverage gap suggests: it is the register that
# decides what a Flash Erase Length means, and its Flash Block Erase Size field
# is the one that reads almost identically to a completely different field at
# 044h.
#
# --- 1. Offset 004h, Device Identification ---
#
#   21     GET_CONFIGURATION
#   00 04  Address, most significant byte first
#   34     CRC
#
#   08     ACCEPT
#   01 00 00 00   Data, least significant byte first -> 00000001h
#   0F 01  Status
#   07     CRC
#
# Version ID 01h, p.94, which is what this revision of the specification
# advertises. Bits 31:8 are Reserved and read as zero, so they are not rendered.
#
# CRC bytes pasted from tools/crc_ref.py (rule R4).
CMD 21 00 04 34
TAR
RSP 08 01 00 00 00 0F 01 07

# --- 2. Offset 040h, Channel 3 Capabilities and Configurations ---
#
#   Data 9B233A6Fh, on the wire least significant byte first.
#
#   31:24  9Bh    RPMC OP1 Opcode on the 1st RPMC Flash device
#   23:20  2h     RPMC Counter on the 1st RPMC Flash device
#                 "Per RPMC SFDP specification, it is a 0-based number" (p.101),
#                 so 2h is 3 counters. Contrast 044h's Target RPMC Supported,
#                 which the page calls 1-based and where the value IS the count.
#   19:18  0h     Reserved
#   17:16  11b    Flash Sharing Capability Supported -> both schemes supported
#   15     0h     Reserved
#   14:12  011b   Flash Access Channel Maximum Read Request Size -> 256 bytes
#   11     1b     Flash Sharing Mode -> target attached flash sharing
#
#                 THIS BIT IS WHAT PICKS BETWEEN TABLE 16'S TWO ERASE SIZE
#                 LISTS. It is the only statement anywhere of which scheme is in
#                 operation, it is set before the channel comes up, and it never
#                 appears in a packet — which is why a Flash Erase Length is
#                 decoded as both readings at once.
#
#   10:8   010b   Flash Access Channel Maximum Payload Size Selected -> 128 bytes
#   7:5    011b   Flash Access Channel Maximum Payload Size Supported -> 256 bytes
#   4:2    011b   Flash Block Erase Size -> 4 Kbytes and 64 Kbytes
#
#                 An ENCODED VALUE, and 011b is the encoding §4.2.4.1 p.79 says
#                 "is not applicable for Flash Erase in Controller Attached
#                 Flash Sharing". Consistent: a capability of *both* sizes is
#                 not a size a single erase request could name.
#
#                 The near-identically named Target Flash Erase Block Size at
#                 044h bits 15:8 is a bit mask, not an encoded value, and this
#                 fixture and config_flash_rpmc.espi decode the two side by side
#                 so the difference is visible in the output rather than only in
#                 the header comments.
#
#   1      1b     Flash Access Channel Ready
#   0      1b     Flash Access Channel Enable
CMD 21 00 40 EF
TAR
RSP 08 6F 3A 23 9B 0F 01 C9
